(function () {
  const R = window.RES;
  R.addLines("met", "NCERT XII (old) Unit 6", [{"sec": "Occurrence of metals","ids": [77],"t": "Free state: C, S, Au, noble gases; others combined in the crust.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 4,"o": "A few elements like carbon, sulphur, gold and noble gases, occur in free state while others are found in combined forms in the earth’s crust."},{"sec": "Occurrence of metals","ids": [78,79],"t": "**Al: most abundant metal, third most abundant element in the crust (~8.3% by weight).**","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 4,"o": "Among metals, aluminium is the most abundant. In fact, it is the third most abundant element in earth’s crust (8.3% approx. by weight)."},{"sec": "Occurrence of metals","ids": [81],"t": "Gems are impure Al2O3: **ruby (Cr impurity), sapphire (Co impurity).**","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 4,"o": "Many gemstones are impure forms of Al2O3. For example, gems ‘ruby’ and ‘sapphire’ have Cr and Co respectively as impurity."},{"sec": "Occurrence of metals","ids": [82],"t": "Fe: second most abundant metal in the crust.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 4,"o": "Iron is the second most abundant metal in the earth’s crust."},{"sec": "Occurrence of metals","ids": [84,85],"t": "Minerals: natural compounds obtained by mining. Ores: minerals viable as a metal source.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 4,"o": "For obtaining a particular metal, first we look for minerals which are naturally occurring chemical substances in the earth’s crust and are obtained through mining. Out of many minerals in which a metal may be found, only a few are viable to be used as source of that metal. Such minerals are known as ores."},{"sec": "Occurrence of metals","ids": [86],"t": "Table 6.1: Aluminium: Bauxite AlOx(OH)3-2x [where 0 < x < 1], Kaolinite (a form of clay) [Al2(OH)4Si2O5]. Iron: Haematite Fe2O3, Magnetite Fe3O4, Siderite FeCO3, Iron pyrites FeS2.","exc": false,"tab": true,"src": "met","page": 4,"o": "Table 6.1: Aluminium: Bauxite AlOx(OH)3-2x [where 0 < x < 1], Kaolinite (a form of clay) [Al2(OH)4Si2O5]. Iron: Haematite Fe2O3, Magnetite Fe3O4, Siderite FeCO3, Iron pyrites FeS2."},{"sec": "Occurrence of metals","ids": [86],"t": "Table 6.1: Copper: Copper pyrites CuFeS2, Malachite CuCO3·Cu(OH)2, Cuprite Cu2O, Copper glance Cu2S. Zinc: Zinc blende or Sphalerite ZnS, Calamine ZnCO3, Zincite ZnO.","exc": false,"tab": true,"src": "met","page": 4,"o": "Table 6.1: Copper: Copper pyrites CuFeS2, Malachite CuCO3·Cu(OH)2, Cuprite Cu2O, Copper glance Cu2S. Zinc: Zinc blende or Sphalerite ZnS, Calamine ZnCO3, Zincite ZnO."},{"sec": "Occurrence of metals","ids": [88,89],"t": "Al from bauxite. Fe from oxide ores (abundant, no polluting SO2 like iron pyrites).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 5,"o": "For the purpose of extraction, bauxite is chosen for aluminium. For iron, usually the oxide ores which are abundant and do not produce polluting gases (like SO2 that is produced in case of iron pyrites) are taken."},{"sec": "Occurrence of metals","ids": [91],"t": "Metallurgy: whole process of isolating a metal from its ore.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 5,"o": "The entire scientific and technological process used for isolation of the metal from its ore is known as metallurgy."},{"sec": "Occurrence of metals","ids": [94,95],"t": "Gangue: earthy, unwanted material in the ore.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 5,"o": "An ore rarely contains only a desired substance. It is usually contaminated with earthly or undesired materials known as gangue."},{"sec": "Occurrence of metals","ids": [96],"t": "Steps: concentration → isolation from concentrated ore → purification.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 5,"o": "The extraction and isolation of metals from ores involves the following major steps: • Concentration of the ore, • Isolation of the metal from its concentrated ore, and • Purification of the metal. In the following Sections, we shall first describe the various steps for effective concentration of ores."},{"sec": "Occurrence of metals","ids": [98],"t": "Concentration = dressing = benefaction: removing sand, clay etc.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 5,"o": "Removal of the unwanted materials (e.g., sand, clays, etc.) from the ore is known as concentration, dressing or benefaction."},{"sec": "Occurrence of metals","ids": [99],"t": "Ores are graded and crushed first.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 5,"o": "Before proceeding for concentration, ores are graded and crushed to reasonable size."},{"sec": "Occurrence of metals","ids": [160,161],"t": "**Flux + gangue → slag**, which separates more easily than gangue.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 8,"o": "During metallurgy, ‘flux’ is added which combines with ‘gangue’ to form ‘slag’. Slag separates more easily from the ore than the gangue."},{"sec": "Concentration of ores","ids": [100],"t": "Concentration method depends on physical property differences between ore and gangue.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 5,"o": "Concentration of ores involves several steps and selection of these steps depends upon the differences in physical properties of the compound of the metal present and that of the gangue."},{"sec": "Concentration of ores","ids": [103,104],"t": "Hydraulic washing: gravity separation by specific gravity difference.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 5,"o": "Hydraulic washing is based on the difference between specific gravities of the ore and the gangue particles. It is therefore a type of gravity separation."},{"sec": "Concentration of ores","ids": [105,106],"t": "Upward water stream washes off light gangue; heavy ore stays.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 5,"o": "In one such process, an upward stream of running water is used to wash the powdered ore. The lighter gangue particles are washed away and the heavier ore particles are left behind."},{"sec": "Concentration of ores","ids": [107,108],"t": "Magnetic separation: when ore or gangue is magnetic.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 5,"o": "Magnetic separation is based on differences in magnetic properties of the ore components. If either the ore or the gangue is attracted towards magnetic field, then the separation is carried out by this method."},{"sec": "Concentration of ores","ids": [109],"t": "Iron ores are attracted to a magnet, so non-magnetic impurities separate.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 5,"o": "For example iron ores are attracted towards magnet, hence, non–magnetic impurities can be separted from them using magnetic separation."},{"sec": "Concentration of ores","ids": [111,112,113],"t": "**Froth floatation: for sulphide ores.** Powdered ore + water + collectors + froth stabilisers.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 6,"o": "Froth floatation is used for removing gangue from sulphide ores. In this process, a suspension of the powdered ore is made with water. Collectors and froth stabilisers are added to it."},{"sec": "Concentration of ores","ids": [114],"t": "**Collectors (pine oils, fatty acids, xanthates) increase non-wettability of mineral; froth stabilisers (cresols, aniline) stabilise froth.**","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 6,"o": "Collectors (e.g., pine oils, fatty acids, xanthates, etc.) enhance nonwettability of the mineral particles and froth stabilisers (e.g., cresols, aniline) stabilise the froth."},{"sec": "Concentration of ores","ids": [115],"t": "Mineral wetted by oil, gangue by water.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 6,"o": "The mineral particles become wet by oils while the gangue particles by water."},{"sec": "Concentration of ores","ids": [116,117,118],"t": "Rotating paddle draws in air; froth carries mineral and is skimmed off.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 6,"o": "A rotating paddle agitates the mixture and draws air in it. As a result, froth is formed which carries the mineral particles. The froth is light and is skimmed off."},{"sec": "Concentration of ores","ids": [120],"t": "Two sulphide ores can be separated by oil:water ratio or depressants.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 6,"o": "Sometimes, it is possible to separate two sulphide ores by adjusting proportion of oil to water or by using ‘depressants’."},{"sec": "Concentration of ores","ids": [121],"t": "**ZnS + PbS: depressant NaCN keeps ZnS out of the froth; PbS goes with the froth.**","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 6,"o": "For example, in the case of an ore containing ZnS and PbS, the depressant used is NaCN. It selectively prevents ZnS from coming to the froth but allows PbS to come with the froth."},{"sec": "Concentration of ores","ids": [376],"t": "Magnetic concentration works for iron ores: haematite, magnetite, siderite, iron pyrites.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 21,"o": "Ores in which one of the components (either the impurity or the actual ore) is magnetic can be concentrated, e.g., ores containing iron (haematite, magnetite, siderite and iron pyrites)."},{"sec": "Leaching","ids": [122],"t": "Leaching: ore soluble in a suitable solvent.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 6,"o": "Leaching is often used if the ore is soluble in some suitable solvent."},{"sec": "Leaching","ids": [123,124],"t": "Bauxite impurities: SiO2, iron oxides, TiO2.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 6,"o": "Bauxite is the principal ore of aluminium. It usually contains SiO2, iron oxides and titanium oxide (TiO2) as impurities."},{"sec": "Leaching","ids": [125,126,127],"t": "**Digestion: bauxite + conc. NaOH at 473–523 K, 35–36 bar → sodium aluminate.**","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 6,"o": "Concentration is carried out by heating the powdered ore with a concentrated solution of NaOH at 473 – 523 K and 35 – 36 bar pressure. This process is called digestion. This way, Al2O3 is extracted out as sodium aluminate."},{"sec": "Leaching","ids": [138],"t": "SiO2 also dissolves as sodium silicate.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 7,"o": "The impurity, SiO2 too dissolves forming sodium silicate."},{"sec": "Leaching","ids": [140],"t": "Al2O3(s) + 2NaOH(aq) + 3H2O(l) → 2Na[Al(OH)4](aq)","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 7,"o": "Al2O3(s) + 2NaOH(aq) + 3H2O(l) → 2Na[Al(OH)4](aq)"},{"sec": "Leaching","ids": [140],"t": "CO2 neutralises aluminate → hydrated Al2O3 precipitates.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 7,"o": "The sodium aluminate present in solution is neutralised by passing CO2 gas and hydrated Al2O3 is precipitated."},{"sec": "Leaching","ids": [141,142,143],"t": "**Seeding: a little fresh hydrated Al2O3 added to induce precipitation.**","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 7,"o": "At this stage, small amount of freshly prepared sample of hydrated Al2O3 is added to the solution. This is called seeding. It induces the precipitation."},{"sec": "Leaching","ids": [143],"t": "2Na[Al(OH)4](aq) + 2CO2(g) → Al2O3·xH2O(s) + 2NaHCO3(aq)","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 7,"o": "2Na[Al(OH)4](aq) + 2CO2(g) → Al2O3·xH2O(s) + 2NaHCO3(aq)"},{"sec": "Leaching","ids": [143],"t": "Sodium silicate stays in solution; hydrated alumina filtered, dried, heated: Al2O3·xH2O(s) →(1470 K) Al2O3(s) + xH2O(g)","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 7,"o": "Sodium silicate remains in the solution and hydrated alumina is filtered, dried and heated to give back pure Al2O3. Al2O3·xH2O(s) →(1470 K) Al2O3(s) + xH2O(g)"},{"sec": "Leaching","ids": [143],"t": "**Ag, Au: leached with dilute NaCN/KCN in air (O2)**; metal recovered by displacement.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 7,"o": "In the metallurgy of silver and gold, the respective metal is leached with a dilute solution of NaCN or KCN in the presence of air, which supplies O2. The metal is obtained later by replacement reaction."},{"sec": "Leaching","ids": [143],"t": "4M(s) + 8CN^–(aq) + 2H2O(aq) + O2(g) → 4[M(CN)2]^–(aq) + 4OH^–(aq) (M = Ag or Au); 2[M(CN)2]^–(aq) + Zn(s) → [Zn(CN)4]^2–(aq) + 2M(s)","exc": false,"tab": true,"src": "met","page": 7,"o": "4M(s) + 8CN^–(aq) + 2H2O(aq) + O2(g) → 4[M(CN)2]^–(aq) + 4OH^–(aq) (M = Ag or Au); 2[M(CN)2]^–(aq) + Zn(s) → [Zn(CN)4]^2–(aq) + 2M(s)"},{"sec": "Leaching","ids": [376],"t": "Leaching removes SiO2, Fe2O3 etc. from bauxite.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 21,"o": "Leaching is significant as it helps in removing the impurities like SiO2, Fe2O3, etc. from the bauxite ore."},{"sec": "Conversion to oxide","ids": [143],"t": "Sulphide ores → oxides before reduction (oxides easier to reduce).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 7,"o": "Usually sulphide ores are converted to oxide before reduction because oxides are easier to reduce."},{"sec": "Conversion to oxide","ids": [144,145],"t": "**Calcination: heating, volatile matter leaves:** Fe2O3·xH2O → Fe2O3 + xH2O; ZnCO3 → ZnO + CO2; CaCO3·MgCO3 → CaO + MgO + 2CO2","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 7,"o": "Calcination involves heating. It removes the volatile matter which escapes leaving behind the metal oxide: Fe2O3·xH2O(s) →Δ Fe2O3(s) + xH2O(g); ZnCO3(s) →Δ ZnO(s) + CO2(g); CaCO3·MgCO3(s) →Δ CaO(s) + MgO(s) + 2CO2(g)"},{"sec": "Conversion to oxide","ids": [145],"t": "**Roasting: heating in regular air supply, below the metal's m.p.**","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 7,"o": "In roasting, the ore is heated in a regular supply of air in a furnace at a temperature below the melting point of the metal."},{"sec": "Conversion to oxide","ids": [146],"t": "2ZnS + 3O2 → 2ZnO + 2SO2; 2PbS + 3O2 → 2PbO + 2SO2; 2Cu2S + 3O2 → 2Cu2O + 2SO2","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 8,"o": "2ZnS + 3O2 → 2ZnO + 2SO2; 2PbS + 3O2 → 2PbO + 2SO2; 2Cu2S + 3O2 → 2Cu2O + 2SO2"},{"sec": "Conversion to oxide","ids": [146,147],"t": "Cu sulphide ores: reverberatory furnace; silica added if iron is present.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 8,"o": "The sulphide ores of copper are heated in reverberatory furnace. If the ore contains iron, it is mixed with silica before heating."},{"sec": "Conversion to oxide","ids": [148],"t": "Iron oxide slags off as FeSiO3; Cu comes out as **matte (Cu2S + FeS)**. FeO + SiO2 → FeSiO3. SO2 used for H2SO4.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 8,"o": "Iron oxide ‘slags of’ as iron silicate and copper is produced in the form of copper matte which contains Cu2S and FeS. FeO + SiO2 → FeSiO3 (slag). The SO2 produced is utilised for manufacturing H2SO4."},{"sec": "Thermodynamic principles","ids": [149,150],"t": "Reduction: heat oxide with C, CO or another metal: MxOy + yC → xM + yCO","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 8,"o": "Reduction of the metal oxide usually involves heating it with a reducing agent, for example C, or CO or even another metal. The reducing agent (e.g., carbon) combines with the oxygen of the metal oxide. MxOy + yC → xM + yCO"},{"sec": "Thermodynamic principles","ids": [153,154],"t": "Gibbs energy decides temperature and choice of reducing agent.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 8,"o": "Gibbs energy is the most significant term. To understand the variation in the temperature required for thermal reductions and to predict which element will suit as the reducing agent for a given metal oxide (MxOy), Gibbs energy interpretations are made."},{"sec": "Thermodynamic principles","ids": [155],"t": "Thermal reduction feasible only if ∆G < 0 at that temperature.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 8,"o": "The criterion for the feasibility of a thermal reduction is that at a given temperture Gibbs energy change of the reaction must be negative."},{"sec": "Thermodynamic principles","ids": [156],"t": "∆G = ∆H – T∆S","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 8,"o": "∆G = ∆H – T∆S, where, ∆H is the enthalpy change and ∆S is the entropy change for the process."},{"sec": "Thermodynamic principles","ids": [157,158,159],"t": "When ∆G is negative, only then the reaction will proceed. ∆G can become negative: (1) if ∆S is positive, on increasing T the value of T∆S increases so that ∆H < T∆S; (2) if coupling of the two reactions, i.e. reduction and oxidation, results in negative value of ∆G for overall reaction.","exc": false,"tab": true,"src": "met","page": 8,"o": "When ∆G is negative, only then the reaction will proceed. ∆G can become negative: (1) if ∆S is positive, on increasing T the value of T∆S increases so that ∆H < T∆S; (2) if coupling of the two reactions, i.e. reduction and oxidation, results in negative value of ∆G for overall reaction."},{"sec": "Thermodynamic principles","ids": [158],"t": "∆S > 0: raising T makes T∆S > ∆H, ∆G goes negative.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 8,"o": "If ∆S is positive, on increasing the temperature (T), the value of T∆S increases so that ∆H < T∆S. In this situation ∆G will become negative on increasing temperature."},{"sec": "Thermodynamic principles","ids": [159],"t": "Coupling reduction with an oxidation can make overall ∆G negative.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 8,"o": "If coupling of the two reactions, i.e. reduction and oxidation, results in negative value of ∆G for overall reaction, the final reaction becomes feasible."},{"sec": "Thermodynamic principles","ids": [164,165],"t": "Plots are per mole of O2 consumed (Ellingham).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 9,"o": "These plots are drawn for free energy changes that occur when one gram mole of oxygen is consumed. The graphical representation of Gibbs energy was first used by H.J.T. Ellingham."},{"sec": "Thermodynamic principles","ids": [166,167,168],"t": "Ellingham diagram: basis for choosing reducing agent; predicts feasibility of thermal reduction.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 9,"o": "This provides a sound basis for considering the choice of reducing agent in the reduction of oxides. This is known as Ellingham Diagram. Such diagrams help us in predicting the feasibility of thermal reduction of an ore."},{"sec": "Thermodynamic principles","ids": [169],"t": "In reduction the oxide decomposes and the reductant takes the oxygen.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 9,"o": "As we know, during reduction, the oxide of a metal decomposes and the reducing agent takes away the oxygen."},{"sec": "Thermodynamic principles","ids": [170],"t": "Reductant's oxidation must have ∆rG° negative enough that the sum of both reactions is negative.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 9,"o": "The role of reducing agent is to provide ∆rG° negative and large enough to make the sum of ∆rG° of the two reactions, i.e, oxidation of the reducing agent and reduction of the metal oxide negative."},{"sec": "Thermodynamic principles","ids": [171],"t": "MxO(s) → xM (solid or liq) + ½O2(g); with carbon as reducing agent: C(s) + ½O2(g) → CO(g); complete oxidation: ½C(s) + ½O2(g) → ½CO2(g).","exc": false,"tab": true,"src": "met","page": 9,"o": "MxO(s) → xM (solid or liq) + ½O2(g); with carbon as reducing agent: C(s) + ½O2(g) → CO(g); complete oxidation: ½C(s) + ½O2(g) → ½CO2(g)."},{"sec": "Thermodynamic principles","ids": [172],"t": "Coupling gives MxO(s) + C(s) → xM(s or l) + CO(g) and MxO(s) + ½C(s) → xM(s or l) + ½CO2(g).","exc": false,"tab": true,"src": "met","page": 10,"o": "Coupling gives MxO(s) + C(s) → xM(s or l) + CO(g) and MxO(s) + ½C(s) → xM(s or l) + ½CO2(g)."},{"sec": "Thermodynamic principles","ids": [173],"t": "Oxide formation consumes gas → ∆S negative → –T∆S term positive.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 10,"o": "In this reaction, gas is consumed in the formation of oxide hence, molecular randomness decreases in the formation of oxide which leades to a negative value of ∆S as a result sign of T∆S term in equation (6.14) becomes positive."},{"sec": "Thermodynamic principles","ids": [174],"t": "So ∆fG° rises with T: **positive slope for most MxO formation lines.**","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 10,"o": "Subsequently ∆fG° shifts towards higher side despite rising T. The result is positive slope in the curve for most of the reactions for the formation of MxO(s)."},{"sec": "Thermodynamic principles","ids": [175,176],"t": "Straight lines sloping up; **slope increases at a phase change** (e.g. Zn melting kink in the Zn–ZnO line).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 10,"o": "Each plot is a straight line and slopes upwards except when some change in phase (s→l or l→g) takes place. The temperature at which such change occurs, is indicated by an increase in the slope on positive side (e.g., in the Zn, ZnO plot, the melting is indicated by an abrupt change in the curve)."},{"sec": "Thermodynamic principles","ids": [177,178,179,180],"t": "Where a line crosses ∆rG° = 0: below, MxO is stable; above, it decomposes on its own.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 10,"o": "When temperature is raised, a point is reached in the curve where it crosses ∆rG° = 0 line. Below this temperature, ∆rG° for the formation of oxide is negative so MxO is stable. Above this point, free energy of formation of oxide is positive. The oxide, MxO will decompose on its own."},{"sec": "Thermodynamic principles","ids": [181,182],"t": "**Similar diagrams for sulphides and halides show why MxS reduction is difficult.**","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 10,"o": "Similar diagrams are constructed for sulfides and halides also. From them it becomes clear that why reduction of MxS is difficult."},{"sec": "Thermodynamic principles","ids": [184,185,186],"t": "**Ellingham shows only feasibility (thermodynamics), not kinetics.**","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 10,"o": "The graph simply indicates whether a reaction is possible or not, i.e., the tendency of reduction with a reducing agent is indicated. This is so because it is based only on the thermodynamic concepts. It does not explain the kinetics of the reduction process."},{"sec": "Thermodynamic principles","ids": [188],"t": "Explains sluggish all-solid reactions vs smooth reaction once ore melts.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 10,"o": "However, it explains why the reactions are sluggish when every species is in solid state and smooth when the ore melts down."},{"sec": "Thermodynamic principles","ids": [189,190],"t": "∆H, ∆S nearly constant with T, so T dominates; but ∆S depends strongly on physical state.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 10,"o": "It is interesting to note here that ∆H (enthalpy change) and the ∆S (entropy change) values for any chemical reaction remain nearly constant even on varying temperature. So the only dominant variable in equation(6.14) becomes T. However, ∆S depends much on the physical state of the compound."},{"sec": "Thermodynamic principles","ids": [191],"t": "Melting (s → l) or vaporising (l → g) increases entropy.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 10,"o": "Since entropy depends on disorder or randomness in the system, it will increase if a compound melts (s→ l) or vapourises (l→ g) since molecular randomness increases on changing the phase from solid to liquid or from liquid to gas. 2."},{"sec": "Thermodynamic principles","ids": [192,193],"t": "**Limitation: ∆rG° interpretation assumes equilibrium (via K), not always true with solids.**","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 10,"o": "The interpretation of ∆rG° is based on K (∆G° = – RT lnK). Thus it is presumed that the reactants and products are in equilibrium. This is not always true because the reactant/product may be solid."},{"sec": "Thermodynamic principles","ids": [197,198],"t": "T chosen so the sum of ∆rG of the coupled redox steps is negative.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 11,"o": "As we have seen, heating (i.e., increasing T) favours a negative value of ∆rG . Therefore, the temperature is chosen such that the sum of ∆rG in the two combined redox processes is negative."},{"sec": "Thermodynamic principles","ids": [199],"t": "That's the intersection of the MxO line with the reductant's oxide line.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 11,"o": "In ∆rG vs T plots (Ellingham diagram), this is indicated by the point of intersection of the two curves, i.e, the curve for the formation of MxO and that for the formation of the oxide of the reducing substance."},{"sec": "Thermodynamic principles","ids": [200],"t": "Beyond the intersection, the combined ∆rG is more negative, reduction possible.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 11,"o": "After that point, the ∆rG value becomes more negative for the combined process making the reduction of MxO possible."},{"sec": "Thermodynamic principles","ids": [201,202],"t": "Element on the lower line can reduce oxide of the upper line; bigger gap → easier.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 11,"o": "The difference in the two ∆rG values after that point determines whether reduction of the oxide of the element of the upper line is feasible by the element of which oxide formation is represented by the lower line. If the difference is large, the reduction is easier."},{"sec": "Thermodynamic principles","ids": [205],"t": "**At point A (Al2O3/MgO crossing), ∆rG = 0 for Al2O3 + 2Mg → 2MgO + Al; below it Mg can reduce alumina.**","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 11,"o": "At the point of intersection of the Al2O3 and MgO curves (marked “A”), the ∆rG becomes ZERO for the reaction: Al2O3 + 2Mg → 2MgO + Al. Below that point magnesium can reduce alumina."},{"sec": "Thermodynamic principles","ids": [206,208,209],"t": "**Mg isn't used for alumina in practice: feasible but uneconomical.**","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 11,"o": "Although thermodynamically feasible, in practice, magnesium metal is not used for the reduction of alumina in the metallurgy of aluminium. Temperatures below the point of intersection of Al2O3 and MgO curves, magnesium can reduce alumina. But the process will be uneconomical."},{"sec": "Thermodynamic principles","ids": [210,211,212],"t": "Liquid metal product → higher ∆S → ∆rG° more negative → easier reduction.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 11,"o": "The entropy is higher if the metal is in liquid state than when it is in solid state. The value of entropy change (∆S) of the reduction process is more on positive side when the metal formed is in liquid state and the metal oxide being reduced is in solid state. Thus the value of ∆rG° becomes more on negative side and the reduction becomes easier."},{"sec": "Thermodynamic principles","ids": [376],"t": "Even feasible reactions need activation energy, so heating is required.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 21,"o": "Certain amount of activation energy is essential even for such reactions which are thermodynamically feasible, therefore heating is required."},{"sec": "Thermodynamic principles","ids": [376],"t": "**Below 1350 °C Mg reduces Al2O3; above 1350 °C Al reduces MgO.**","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 21,"o": "Yes, below 1350°C Mg can reduce Al2O3 and above 1350°C, Al can reduce MgO. This can be inferred from ∆G° vs T plots."},{"sec": "Extraction of iron","ids": [203],"t": "Fe oxide ores (Fe2O3, Fe3O4): calcination/roasting removes water, decomposes carbonates, oxidises sulphides.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 11,"o": "After concentration, mixture of oxide ores of iron (Fe2O3, Fe3O4) is subjected to calcination/roasting to remove water, to decompose carbonates and to oxidise sulphides."},{"sec": "Extraction of iron","ids": [204],"t": "Mixed with limestone + coke, fed into the blast furnace from the top.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 11,"o": "After that these are mixed with limestone and coke and fed into a Blast furnace from its top, in which the oxide is reduced to the metal."},{"sec": "Extraction of iron","ids": [214],"t": "Hot air blast from the bottom burns coke → ~2200 K; coke supplies most of the heat.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 12,"o": "A blast of hot air is blown from the bottom of the furnace by burning coke in the lower portion to give temperature upto about 2200K. The burning of coke, therefore, supplies most of the heat required in the process."},{"sec": "Extraction of iron","ids": [215],"t": "CO and heat move up the furnace.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 12,"o": "The CO and heat move to the upper part of the furnace."},{"sec": "Extraction of iron","ids": [216],"t": "**500–800 K (lower range):** 3Fe2O3 + CO → 2Fe3O4 + CO2; Fe3O4 + 4CO → 3Fe + 4CO2; Fe2O3 + CO → 2FeO + CO2","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 12,"o": "At 500 – 800 K (lower temperature range in the blast furnace), Fe2O3 is first reduced to Fe3O4 and then to FeO: 3Fe2O3 + CO → 2Fe3O4 + CO2; Fe3O4 + 4CO → 3Fe + 4CO2; Fe2O3 + CO → 2FeO + CO2"},{"sec": "Extraction of iron","ids": [216,217],"t": "Limestone → CaO, removes silicate impurity as molten slag.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 12,"o": "Limestone is also decomposed to CaO which removes silicate impurity of the ore as slag. The slag is in molten state and separates out from iron."},{"sec": "Extraction of iron","ids": [218],"t": "**900–1500 K (higher range):** C + CO2 → 2CO; FeO + CO → Fe + CO2","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 12,"o": "At 900 – 1500 K (higher temperature range in the blast furnace): C + CO2 → 2CO; FeO + CO → Fe + CO2"},{"sec": "Extraction of iron","ids": [219,220],"t": "Key step FeO(s) + C(s) → Fe(s/l) + CO(g): two coupled reactions.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 12,"o": "One of the main reduction steps in this process involves reaction 6.27 given below. FeO(s) + C(s) → Fe(s/l) + CO (g) (6.27) This reaction can be seen as a reaction in which two simpler reactions have coupled."},{"sec": "Extraction of iron","ids": [221],"t": "FeO(s) → Fe(s) + ½O2(g); C(s) + ½O2(g) → CO(g). Net: ∆rG°(C, CO) + ∆rG°(FeO, Fe) = ∆rG°; the resultant reaction will take place when the right hand side is negative.","exc": false,"tab": true,"src": "met","page": 12,"o": "FeO(s) → Fe(s) + ½O2(g); C(s) + ½O2(g) → CO(g). Net: ∆rG°(C, CO) + ∆rG°(FeO, Fe) = ∆rG°; the resultant reaction will take place when the right hand side is negative."},{"sec": "Extraction of iron","ids": [222,223],"t": "**Fe → FeO line goes up, C → CO line goes down; they cross at ~1073 K.**","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 12,"o": "In ∆rG vs T plot representing the change Fe→FeO goes upward and that representing the change C→CO (C,CO) goes downward. They cross each other at about 1073K."},{"sec": "Extraction of iron","ids": [224],"t": "**Above 1073 K (900–1500 K), coke reduces FeO, itself oxidised to CO.**","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 12,"o": "So above 1073 K in the range of temprature 900–1500 K coke will reduce FeO and will itself be oxidised to CO."},{"sec": "Extraction of iron","ids": [225,226,227],"t": "At ~1673 K: 2FeO → 2Fe + O2 +341, 2C + O2 → 2CO –447 → overall –53 kJ mol^–1.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 12,"o": "At about 1673K (1400 °C) ∆rG value for the reaction 2FeO → 2Fe + O2 is +341 kJ mol^-1 and for the reaction 2C + O2 → 2CO ∆rG is -447 kJ mol^-1. If we calculate ∆rG value for overall reaction the value will be -53 kJ mol^-1."},{"sec": "Extraction of iron","ids": [228],"t": "So FeO + C becomes feasible.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 13,"o": "Therefore, reaction 6.27 becomes feasible."},{"sec": "Extraction of iron","ids": [229],"t": "Fe3O4 and Fe2O3 are reduced by CO at lower T: their lines cross the CO–CO2 line lower.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 13,"o": "In a similar way the reduction of Fe3O4 and Fe2O3 by CO at relatively lower temperatures can be explained on the basis of lower lying points of intersection of their curves with the CO, CO2 curve."},{"sec": "Extraction of iron","ids": [230,231],"t": "**Pig iron: ~4% C + small S, P, Si, Mn.**","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 13,"o": "The iron obtained from Blast furnace contains about 4% carbon and many impurities in smaller amount (e.g., S, P, Si, Mn). This is known as pig iron."},{"sec": "Extraction of iron","ids": [233],"t": "**Cast iron: pig iron + scrap iron + coke with hot air; ~3% C, extremely hard and brittle.**","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 13,"o": "Cast iron is different from pig iron and is made by melting pig iron with scrap iron and coke using hot air blast. It has slightly lower carbon content (about 3%) and is extremely hard and brittle."},{"sec": "Extraction of iron","ids": [234],"t": "**Wrought (malleable) iron: purest commercial iron**, from cast iron in a haematite-lined reverberatory furnace.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 13,"o": "Wrought iron or malleable iron is the purest form of commercial iron and is prepared from cast iron by oxidising impurities in a reverberatory furnace lined with haematite."},{"sec": "Extraction of iron","ids": [235],"t": "Haematite oxidises C: Fe2O3 + 3C → 2Fe + 3CO. Limestone flux; S, Si, P oxidised into slag.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 13,"o": "The haematite oxidises carbon to carbon monoxide: Fe2O3 + 3C → 2Fe + 3CO. Limestone is added as a flux and sulphur, silicon and phosphorus are oxidised and passed into the slag."},{"sec": "Extraction of copper","ids": [237,238],"t": "**Cu2O line is near the top of the Ellingham diagram: easily reduced by coke.**","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 13,"o": "In the graph of ∆rG vs T for the formation of oxides, the Cu2O line is almost at the top. So it is quite easy to reduce oxide ores of copper directly to the metal by heating with coke."},{"sec": "Extraction of copper","ids": [239],"t": "(C, CO) and (C, CO2) lines are much lower, especially after 500–600 K. But most Cu ores are sulphides, some with iron.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 13,"o": "The lines (C, CO) and (C, CO2) are at much lower positions in the graph particularly after 500 – 600K. However, many of the ores are sulphides and some may also contain iron."},{"sec": "Extraction of copper","ids": [240],"t": "Roast/smelt: 2Cu2S + 3O2 → 2Cu2O + 2SO2; then Cu2O + C → 2Cu + CO","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 13,"o": "The sulphide ores are roasted/smelted to give oxides: 2Cu2S + 3O2 → 2Cu2O + 2SO2. The oxide can then be easily reduced to metallic copper using coke: Cu2O + C → 2Cu + CO"},{"sec": "Extraction of copper","ids": [240,241,242],"t": "Actual process: reverberatory furnace with silica; iron slags off as silicate; copper matte (Cu2S + FeS) forms.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 13,"o": "In actual process, the ore is heated in a reverberatory furnace after mixing with silica. In the furnace, iron oxide ‘slags of’ as iron slicate is formed. Copper is produced in the form of copper matte. This contains Cu2S and FeS."},{"sec": "Extraction of copper","ids": [242,243,244],"t": "Matte → silica-lined converter, more silica, hot air blast → metallic copper.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 13,"o": "Copper matte is then charged into silica lined convertor. Some silica is also added and hot air blast is blown to convert the remaining FeS, FeO and Cu2S/Cu2O to the metallic copper."},{"sec": "Extraction of copper","ids": [245],"t": "2FeS + 3O2 → 2FeO + 2SO2; FeO + SiO2 → FeSiO3; 2Cu2S + 3O2 → 2Cu2O + 2SO2; 2Cu2O + Cu2S → 6Cu + SO2","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 14,"o": "2FeS + 3O2 → 2FeO + 2SO2; FeO + SiO2 → FeSiO3; 2Cu2S + 3O2 → 2Cu2O + 2SO2; 2Cu2O + Cu2S → 6Cu + SO2"},{"sec": "Extraction of copper","ids": [245],"t": "**Blister copper: blistered look from escaping SO2.**","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 14,"o": "The solidified copper obtained has blistered appearance due to the evolution of SO2 and so it is called blister copper."},{"sec": "Extraction of copper","ids": [364],"t": "**This is self-reduction in the converter.**","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 19,"o": "It is self reduction in a specially designed converter."},{"sec": "Extraction of copper","ids": [366],"t": "H2SO4 leaching for low-grade ores (hydrometallurgy).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 19,"o": "Sulphuric acid leaching is also used in hydrometallurgy for low grade ores."},{"sec": "Extraction of zinc","ids": [246,247],"t": "ZnO reduced by coke, at higher T than for Cu.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 14,"o": "The reduction of zinc oxide is done using coke. The temperature in this case is higher than that in the case of copper."},{"sec": "Extraction of zinc","ids": [248,249],"t": "Brickettes of ZnO + coke + clay: ZnO + C →(coke, 1673 K) Zn + CO. **Zn distilled off, collected by rapid chilling.**","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 14,"o": "For the purpose of heating, the oxide is made into brickettes with coke and clay. ZnO + C →(coke, 1673 K) Zn + CO. The metal is distilled off and collected by rapid chilling."},{"sec": "Extraction of zinc","ids": [251,252,253],"t": "Ions in solution/melt: reduced by electrolysis or a reducing element; molten salts are electrolysed.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 14,"o": "Similar principles are effective in the reductions of metal ions in solution or molten state. Here they are reduced by electrolysis or by adding some reducing element. In the reduction of a molten metal salt, electrolysis is done."},{"sec": "Extraction of zinc","ids": [367],"t": "Zn can be purified by fractional distillation.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 19,"o": "The metal may be purified by fractional distillation."},{"sec": "Electrochemical principles","ids": [254],"t": "∆G = –nE°F","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 14,"o": "∆G = – nE°F, here n is the number of electrons and E° is the electrode potential of the redox couple formed in the system."},{"sec": "Electrochemical principles","ids": [255],"t": "More reactive metals: large negative E°, hard to reduce.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 14,"o": "More reactive metals have large negative values of the electrode potential. So their reduction is difficult."},{"sec": "Electrochemical principles","ids": [256],"t": "Positive E°(cell) → negative ∆G: less reactive metal comes out of solution: Cu^2+(aq) + Fe(s) → Cu(s) + Fe^2+(aq)","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 14,"o": "If the difference of two E° values corresponds to a positive E° and consequently negative ∆G, then the less reactive metal will come out of the solution and the more reactive metal will go into the solution, e.g., Cu^2+(aq) + Fe(s) → Cu(s) + Fe^2+(aq)"},{"sec": "Electrochemical principles","ids": [257],"t": "Electrodes chosen considering reactivity of the metal produced.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 14,"o": "Precautions are taken considering the reactivity of the metal produced and suitable materials are used as electrodes."},{"sec": "Electrochemical principles","ids": [258],"t": "Flux sometimes added to make the melt more conducting. Cr2O3 + 2Al → Al2O3 + 2Cr (∆rG° = –421 kJ) is thermodynamically feasible.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 14,"o": "Sometimes a flux is added for making the molten mass more conducting. 6.3 The reaction, Cr2O3+2Al → Al2O3+2Cr (∆rG0 = – 421kJ) is thermodynamically feasible as is apparent from the Gibbs energy value."},{"sec": "Electrochemical principles","ids": [261,262],"t": "**Al: purified Al2O3 + Na3AlF6 or CaF2 (lower m.p., add conductivity)**, fused matrix electrolysed.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 15,"o": "In the metallurgy of aluminium, purified Al2O3 is mixed with Na3AlF6 or CaF2 which lowers the melting point of the mixture and brings conductivity. The fused matrix is electrolysed."},{"sec": "Electrochemical principles","ids": [263,264],"t": "**Hall–Héroult: carbon-lined steel cathode, graphite anode:** 2Al2O3 + 3C → 4Al + 3CO2","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 15,"o": "Steel vessel with lining of carbon acts as cathode and graphite anode is used. The overall reaction may be written as: 2Al2O3 + 3C → 4Al + 3CO2. This process of electrolysis is widely known as Hall-Heroult process."},{"sec": "Electrochemical principles","ids": [265],"t": "Carbon electrodes, electrolytic cell, molten mass.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 15,"o": "Thus electrolysis of the molten mass is carried out in an electrolytic cell using carbon electrodes."},{"sec": "Electrochemical principles","ids": [266],"t": "**Anode O burns the carbon to CO and CO2: ~0.5 kg anode per kg Al.**","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 15,"o": "The oxygen liberated at anode reacts with the carbon of anode producing CO and CO2. This way for each kg of aluminium produced, about 0.5 kg of carbon anode is burnt away."},{"sec": "Electrochemical principles","ids": [267],"t": "Cathode: Al^3+(melt) + 3e^– → Al(l); Anode: C(s) + O^2–(melt) → CO(g) + 2e^–; C(s) + 2O^2–(melt) → CO2(g) + 4e^–","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 15,"o": "Cathode: Al^3+(melt) + 3e^– → Al(l); Anode: C(s) + O^2–(melt) → CO(g) + 2e^–; C(s) + 2O^2–(melt) → CO2(g) + 4e^–"},{"sec": "Electrochemical principles","ids": [267,268,269],"t": "Cu hydrometallurgy (low-grade ores): leach with acid or bacteria; Cu^2+ treated with scrap iron or H2.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 15,"o": "Copper is extracted by hydrometallurgy from low grade ores. It is leached out using acid or bacteria. The solution containing Cu^2+ is treated with scrap iron or H2."},{"sec": "Electrochemical principles","ids": [270],"t": "Cu^2+(aq) + H2(g) → Cu(s) + 2H^+(aq)","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 15,"o": "Cu^2+(aq) + H2(g) → Cu(s) + 2H^+(aq)"},{"sec": "Electrochemical principles","ids": [276,277],"t": "Zn scrap would reduce Cu^2+ faster (more reactive), but iron scrap is cheaper and preferred.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 15,"o": "Zinc being above iron in the electrochemical series (more reactive metal is zinc), the reduction will be faster in case zinc scraps are used. But zinc is costlier metal than iron so using iron scraps will be advisable and advantageous."},{"sec": "Electrochemical principles","ids": [342],"t": "Electrode potential is used in isolating Al, Ag, Au: sum of redox couples positive → ∆G negative.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 19,"o": "The concept of electrode potential is useful in the isolation of metals (e.g., Al, Ag, Au) where the sum of the two redox couples is positive so that the Gibbs energy change is negative."},{"sec": "Oxidation reduction","ids": [270,271],"t": "Some extractions use oxidation, mainly for non-metals: **Cl2 from brine.**","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 15,"o": "Besides reductions, some extractions are based on oxidation particularly for non-metals. A very common example of extraction based on oxidation is the extraction of chlorine from brine."},{"sec": "Oxidation reduction","ids": [271],"t": "2Cl^– + 2H2O → 2OH^– + H2 + Cl2; ∆G = +422 kJ → E° = –2.2 V.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 15,"o": "2Cl^–(aq) + 2H2O(l) → 2OH^–(aq) + H2(g) + Cl2(g). The ∆G for this reaction is + 422 kJ. When it is converted to E° (using ∆G° = – nE°F), we get E° = – 2.2 V."},{"sec": "Oxidation reduction","ids": [271],"t": "Needs external emf > 2.2 V, plus excess to overcome hindering reactions.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 15,"o": "Naturally, it will require an external emf that is greater than 2.2 V. But the electrolysis requires an excess potential to overcome some other hindering reactions."},{"sec": "Oxidation reduction","ids": [272,273,274],"t": "**Brine electrolysis gives Cl2 with H2 and NaOH; molten NaCl gives Na metal, not NaOH.**","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 15,"o": "Thus, Cl2 is obtained by electrolysis giving out H2 and aqueous NaOH as by-products. Electrolysis of molten NaCl is also carried out. But in that case, Na metal is produced and not NaOH."},{"sec": "Oxidation reduction","ids": [278,279],"t": "Au/Ag cyanide leaching is an oxidation (Ag → Ag^+, Au → Au^+).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 16,"o": "As studied earlier, extraction of gold and silver involves leaching the metal with CN^–. This is also an oxidation reaction (Ag → Ag^+ or Au → Au^+)."},{"sec": "Oxidation reduction","ids": [280],"t": "4Au + 8CN^– + 2H2O + O2 → 4[Au(CN)2]^– + 4OH^–; 2[Au(CN)2]^– + Zn → 2Au + [Zn(CN)4]^2–. **Zn is the reductant.**","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 16,"o": "4Au(s) + 8CN^–(aq) + 2H2O(aq) + O2(g) → 4[Au(CN)2]^–(aq) + 4OH^–(aq); 2[Au(CN)2]^–(aq) + Zn(s) → 2Au(s) + [Zn(CN)4]^2–(aq). In this reaction zinc acts as a reducing agent."},{"sec": "Oxidation reduction","ids": [281,282],"t": "Refining method chosen by property difference between metal and impurity.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 16,"o": "A metal extracted by any method is usually contaminated with some impurity. For obtaining metals of high purity, several techniques are used depending upon the differences in properties of the metal and the impurity."},{"sec": "Refining","ids": [284],"t": "Refining: distillation, liquation, electrolysis, zone refining, vapour phase refining, chromatography.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 16,"o": "(a) Distillation (b) Liquation (c) Electrolysis (d) Zone refining (e) Vapour phase refining (f) Chromatographic methods."},{"sec": "Refining","ids": [285,286],"t": "**Distillation: low-boiling Zn, Hg.**","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 16,"o": "Distillation is very useful for low boiling metals like zinc and mercury. The impure metal is evaporated to obtain the pure metal as distillate."},{"sec": "Refining","ids": [287,288],"t": "**Liquation: low-melting Sn flows down a slope**, leaving higher-melting impurities.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 16,"o": "Liquation: in this method a low melting metal like tin can be made to flow on a sloping surface. In this way it is separated from higher melting impurities."},{"sec": "Refining","ids": [289,290,291],"t": "Electrolytic refining: impure anode, pure cathode, salt of same metal; **more basic metals stay in solution, less basic go to anode mud.**","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 16,"o": "In electrolytic refining, the impure metal is made to act as anode. A strip of the same metal in pure form is used as cathode. They are put in a suitable electrolytic bath containing soluble salt of the same metal. The more basic metal remains in the solution and the less basic ones go to the anode mud."},{"sec": "Refining","ids": [293,294],"t": "Cu refined electrolytically: impure Cu anodes, pure Cu cathodes.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 16,"o": "Copper is refined using an electrolytic method. Anodes are of impure copper and pure copper strips are taken as cathode."},{"sec": "Refining","ids": [295],"t": "Acidified CuSO4 electrolyte; Anode: Cu → Cu^2+ + 2e^–; Cathode: Cu^2+ + 2e^– → Cu","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 16,"o": "The electrolyte is acidified solution of copper sulphate and the net result of electrolysis is the transfer of copper in pure form from the anode to the cathode: Anode: Cu → Cu^2+ + 2e^–; Cathode: Cu^2+ + 2e^– → Cu"},{"sec": "Refining","ids": [295],"t": "**Anode mud from blister Cu: Sb, Se, Te, Ag, Au, Pt**; recovery can pay for refining.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 16,"o": "Impurities from the blister copper deposit as anode mud which contains antimony, selenium, tellurium, silver, gold and platinum; recovery of these elements may meet the cost of refining."},{"sec": "Refining","ids": [296],"t": "Zn can also be refined this way.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 16,"o": "Zinc may also be refined this way."},{"sec": "Refining","ids": [297],"t": "**Zone refining: impurities more soluble in the melt than in the solid.** Mobile heater around the impure rod.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 17,"o": "Zone refining is based on the principle that the impurities are more soluble in the melt than in the solid state of the metal. A mobile heater surrounding the rod of impure metal is fixed at its one end."},{"sec": "Refining","ids": [298],"t": "Molten zone moves with the heater.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 17,"o": "The molten zone moves along with the heater which is moved forward."},{"sec": "Refining","ids": [299,300,301,302],"t": "Pure metal crystallises behind; impurities move into the new molten zone; repeated in one direction; impure end cut off.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 17,"o": "As the heater moves forward, the pure metal crystallises out of the melt left behind and the impurities pass on into the adjacent new molten zone created by movement of heaters. The process is repeated several times and the heater is moved in the same direction again and again. Impurities get concentrated at one end. This end is cut off."},{"sec": "Refining","ids": [303],"t": "**For semiconductors and ultra-pure metals: Ge, Si, B, Ga, In.**","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 17,"o": "This method is very useful for producing semiconductor and other metals of very high purity, e.g., germanium, silicon, boron, gallium and indium."},{"sec": "Refining","ids": [304],"t": "Vapour phase refining: metal must form a volatile compound, and it must decompose easily.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 17,"o": "In vapour phase refining, the metal is converted into its volatile compound which is collected and decomposed to give pure metal. So, the two requirements are: (i) the metal should form a volatile compound with an available reagent, (ii) the volatile compound should be easily decomposable, so that the recovery is easy."},{"sec": "Refining","ids": [306,307],"t": "**Mond process (Ni):** Ni + 4CO →(330–350 K) Ni(CO)4; Ni(CO)4 →(450–470 K) Ni + 4CO","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 17,"o": "Mond Process for Refining Nickel: In this process, nickel is heated in a stream of carbon monoxide forming a volatile complex named as nickel tetracarbonyl. This complex is decomposed at higher temperature to obtain pure metal. Ni + 4CO →(330 – 350 K) Ni(CO)4; Ni(CO)4 →(450 – 470 K) Ni + 4CO"},{"sec": "Refining","ids": [307],"t": "**van Arkel (Zr, Ti): removes O and N impurities**; crude metal heated with I2 in an evacuated vessel.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 17,"o": "van Arkel Method for Refining Zirconium or Titanium: This method is very useful for removing all the oxygen and nitrogen present in the form of impurity in certain metals like Zr and Ti. The crude metal is heated in an evacuated vessel with iodine."},{"sec": "Refining","ids": [308,309],"t": "Zr + 2I2 → ZrI4 (covalent, volatile); **decomposed on W filament at ~1800 K:** ZrI4 → Zr + 2I2","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 17,"o": "The metal iodide being more covalent, volatilises: Zr + 2I2 → ZrI4. The metal iodide is decomposed on a tungsten filament, electrically heated to about 1800K. The pure metal deposits on the filament. ZrI4 → Zr + 2I2"},{"sec": "Refining","ids": [320],"t": "Column chromatography: elements in minute amounts with chemically similar impurities.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 18,"o": "Column chromatography is very useful for purification of the elements which are available in minute quantities and the impurities are not very different in chemical properties from the element to be purified."},{"sec": "Uses","ids": [321,322],"t": "Al foil food wrappers; Al dust in paints and lacquers.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 18,"o": "Aluminium foils are used as wrappers for food materials. The fine dust of the metal is used in paints and lacquers."},{"sec": "Uses","ids": [323],"t": "**Al (highly reactive) extracts Cr and Mn from their oxides.**","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 18,"o": "Aluminium, being highly reactive, is also used in the extraction of chromium and manganese from their oxides."},{"sec": "Uses","ids": [326,327],"t": "Cu: electrical wires, water/steam pipes; alloys brass (Zn), bronze (Sn), coinage alloy (Ni).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 18,"o": "Copper is used for making wires used in electrical industry and for water and steam pipes. It is also used in several alloys that are rather tougher than the metal itself, e.g., brass (with zinc), bronze (with tin) and coinage alloy (with nickel)."},{"sec": "Uses","ids": [328,329],"t": "Zn: galvanising iron; batteries.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 18,"o": "Zinc is used for galvanising iron. It is also used in large quantities in batteries."},{"sec": "Uses","ids": [330],"t": "**Brass: Cu 60%, Zn 40%. German silver: Cu 25–30%, Zn 25–30%, Ni 40–50%.**","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 18,"o": "It is constituent of many alloys, e.g., brass, (Cu 60%, Zn 40%) and german silver (Cu 25-30%, Zn 25-30%, Ni 40–50%)."},{"sec": "Uses","ids": [331],"t": "Zn dust: reducing agent in making dyestuffs, paints.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 18,"o": "Zinc dust is used as a reducing agent in the manufacture of dye-stuffs, paints, etc."},{"sec": "Uses","ids": [331],"t": "Cast iron (most important form): stoves, railway sleepers, gutter pipes, toys; makes wrought iron and steel.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 18,"o": "Cast iron, which is the most important form of iron, is used for casting stoves, railway sleepers, gutter pipes, toys, etc. It is used in the manufacture of wrought iron and steel."},{"sec": "Uses","ids": [332],"t": "Wrought iron: anchors, wires, bolts, chains, agricultural implements.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 18,"o": "Wrought iron is used in making anchors, wires, bolts, chains and agricultural implements."},{"sec": "Uses","ids": [335],"t": "Nickel steel: cables, automobile and aeroplane parts, pendulums, measuring tapes.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 18,"o": "Nickel steel is used for making cables, automobiles and aeroplane parts, pendulum, measuring tapes."},{"sec": "Uses","ids": [336],"t": "Chrome steel: cutting tools, crushers. Stainless steel: cycles, automobiles, utensils, pens.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "met","page": 18,"o": "Chrome steel is used for cutting tools and crushing machines, and stainless steel is used for cycles, automobiles, utensils, pens, etc."}]);
})();
